mode: "100644"
text: "# Shot patterns, decision tables & worked examples\n\n## The model decision table (verify — the line moves)\n| Job | Model | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Volume social shots, most work | Ray3.14 | native 1080p, ~4x faster, ~3x cheaper at 720p |\n| Identity-locked character across shots | Ray3 | character reference (3.14 dropped it) |\n| HDR/EXR film delivery, graded finals | Ray3 | 16-bit HDR, EXR export |\n| Restyle real footage, keep performance | Ray3 Modify (V2V) | footage as directing input |\n| Sound-native generation | not Luma | route to veo-3/kling (Luma = sound in post) |\n\n## The shot-brief pattern\n\"[Subject with concrete detail] [action], in [setting]. [Lighting/time]. [Mood]. Camera: [move — slow push-in /\norbit / handheld]. Style: [cinematic reference].\" One shot per brief. Draft it, pick the winner, Hi-Fi it.\n\n## The sequence workflow (a 30-60s brand film)\n1. Script + shot list first (short-form-video-script / scripting skills) — 4-8 shots.\n2. Canonical reference set: brand-true stills (flux/Photon), character refs if any (Ray3).\n3. Per shot: draft iterations → winner → Hi-Fi master; keyframe end/start frames to match cuts.\n4. Assemble + pace in capcut; sound in post (ai-music-and-sound / licensed); captions.\n5. Drift review side-by-side → human approval → WoopSocial.\n\n## The credit-budget worksheet (before subscribing)\nShots needed/mo × ~3 attempts × per-shot rate (model + resolution + SDR/HDR) = credit need. Check the current\nrate table (rates are model/resolution-specific and shift). HDR only for finals. Monthly credits expire —\nschedule generation sessions; top-ups roll over; API wallet funded separately.\n\n## Worked example A — \"Blunt indie founder\" (draft discipline)\n\"burned 4,000 credits my first week rendering everything at 1080p HDR like an amateur. new rule: every idea\ndrafts first. eight drafts cost less than one HDR master. picked the one where the steam actually behaved,\nmastered that single shot, done. same output, a fifth of the spend. hdr is for the final cut, not the\nbrainstorm.\" — draft-first, HDR reserved, honest math.\n\n## Worked example B — \"Warm bookkeeping studio\" (the still-first teaser)\n\"We didn't ask the video model to imagine our brand — we gave it our brand. One photo of our actual desk setup\n(our green, our mug), image-to-video, a gentle push-in, morning light. Three drafts, one winner, mastered once.\nThen we added a soft licensed piano track in the edit, because the clip comes out silent — that's normal, not\nbroken. One small step: start every video from a still you already love.\" — image-to-video, sound-in-post\nexpectation set, brand-true.\n\n## Never\nRender iterations in HDR · skip Draft Mode · promise a one-generation \"film\" · chain Extends past quality ·\nship a silent clip as finished · assume free/cheap tiers cover client work · forget Luma's marketing\nlicense-back on your clips · use an unpermitted likeness or fake-event footage of real entities · treat one\nreview's tier table as fact · claim WoopSocial generates video.\n"
